  "nl_text": "The database schema \"rschema_0028\" is used to store information about a timber company. It consists of five tables: \"timber,\" \"department,\" \"employee,\" \"timber_procurement,\" and \"timber_processing.\"\nThe \"timber\" table contains information about different types of timber, including their names, types, procurement prices, processing costs, and sales prices. The \"timber_id\" column is the primary key and is used to uniquely identify each type of timber.\nThe \"department\" table contains information about the different departments within the company, including their names, supervisors, contact information, and department IDs. The \"department_id\" column is the primary key and is used to uniquely identify each department.\nThe \"employee\" table contains information about the employees within the company, including their names, genders, phone numbers, positions, and department IDs. The \"employee_id\" column is the primary key and is used to uniquely identify each employee.\nThe \"timber_procurement\" table contains information about the procurement of timber, including the type of timber, the quantity procured, the supplier, and the time of procurement. The \"employee_id\" and \"timber_id\" columns are used to link the procurement to the appropriate employee and timber type.\nThe \"timber_processing\" table contains information about the processing of timber, including the type of timber, the quantity processed, and the time of processing. The \"employee_id\" and \"timber_id\" columns are used to link the processing to the appropriate employee and timber type.\nThe schema also includes foreign key constraints to ensure referential integrity, which means that the data in the tables is consistent and accurate. For example, the \"timber_id\" column in the \"timber_procurement\" table references the \"timber_id\" column in the \"timber\" table, ensuring that the procurement of timber is linked to the appropriate timber type. Similarly, the \"employee_id\" column in the \"timber_procurement\" and \"timber_processing\" tables references the \"employee_id\" column in the \"employee\" table, ensuring that the procurement and processing of timber are linked to the appropriate employee.",
  "gold_question": "The timber processing company needs to manage the procurement, processing, and sales of timber. Timber has information such as name, type, purchase price, processing cost, and selling price. Multiple departments in the company collaborate, each department has a name, person in charge, and contact information. Employee management includes employee ID, name, gender, phone number, and position. For each procurement, the system shall record the purchased timber, quantity, purchaser, supplier, and purchase time. During processing, the system shall record the processed timber, quantity, processor, and processing time. For sales, the system shall record the sold timber, quantity, salesperson, customer, and sales time. The system shall support performance evaluation for each department.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_codellama13b/0028.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0028\nCREATE TABLE `timber` (\n  `timber_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `procurement_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `processing_cost` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `sales_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`timber_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `department` (\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `supervisor` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_info` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`department_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `employee` (\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(15) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `position` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`employee_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `employee_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `department` (`department_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `timber_procurement` (\n  `procurement_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `timber_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `supplier` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `procurement_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`procurement_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `timber_procurement_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`timber_id`) REFERENCES `timber` (`timber_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `timber_procurement_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employee` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `timber_processing` (\n  `processing_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `timber_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `processing_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`processing_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `timber_processing_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`timber_id`) REFERENCES `timber` (`timber_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `timber_processing_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employee` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
